ENA recovery regression
=======================

Run `make regress` on OpenBSD, or from the source root:

    sh regress/sys/dev/ena/reset.sh sys/dev/pci/if_ena.c

The test compiles the actual reset task, device recovery function and admin
interrupt handler from if_ena.c against a small HAL/device model. The model
starts with an admin queue that no longer completes commands. Reusing that
queue reproduces the permanent interface-down failure; a device reset and
fresh admin queues are required to recover. It also checks repeat recovery,
failed reset DMA retention, initialization failure and retry, identity
changes, stale reset tasks, and interrupts during admin ring teardown.

Observed failure sequence:

    ena0: keep-alive watchdog timeout, resetting
    ena0: The ena device didn't send a completion for the admin cmd 2
    ena0: Failed to destroy io sq error: 60
    ena0: Failed to create IO CQ. error: 19
    ena0: failed to create IO queues

The first failing command is DESTROY_SQ. Its timeout sets the HAL admin
queue's running_state to false, so CREATE_CQ returns ENODEV without reaching
the device. The old reset task only called ena_stop/ena_init and never reset
the device or rebuilt AQ/ACQ/AENQ. A host reboot restored networking.
The evidence does not identify the cause of the initial missing keep-alive.
Disassembly of the live /bsd also confirmed that its reset task called only
ena_stop and ena_init.

This model does not emulate DMA, MMIO or MSI-X hardware. Native kernel build
and fault injection on a disposable ENA host are separate validation steps.

Hardware regression
-------------------

inject.awk makes a TEST-ONLY copy of if_ena.c that resets the NIC's hardware
without rebuilding its queues at 90 and 180 seconds of uptime. This removes
the admin/AENQ and I/O queues underneath the running driver. The ordinary
keep-alive watchdog must then restore the device and resume network traffic.

    awk -f regress/sys/dev/ena/inject.awk sys/dev/pci/if_ena.c > if_ena.test.c

Use that copy in a separate kernel build tree, never the production source
or a production host. Retain a known-good boot kernel and an independent
recovery path. Monitor traffic from another host through both injections;
check the watchdog messages, ena-txq reset counters, packet counters, and
continued SSH connectivity. Restore the test host's original kernel after
the test. The injector is not part of the normal kernel or `make regress`.

Validation results
------------------

The GENERIC.MP kernel built on native OpenBSD with -Werror, and all eight
model regression cases passed. The updated manual page passed mandoc lint.

On ENA hardware, the patched driver recovered from both queue-loss
injections. All eight transmit queues recorded two resets, packet traffic
resumed, and an established SSH session survived both recoveries.

The original driver, built with the same injector, reproduced the failure
sequence above after the first injection and remained unreachable until an
external reboot. The test host's original boot kernel was restored before
fault injection so that this reboot could recover it.
